    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on some walnut cabinet joinery and I'm running into a frustrating issue. Whenever I'm cutting the dovetails, the wood fibers are splintering right at the tips of the pins. It's ruining the edges and I can't get a clean finish. I've checked my layout, but the splintering is persistent. Any advice on tool sharpness or maybe how I should be managing the grain direction to prevent this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This splintering usually indicates that your tools aren't sharp enough for the demanding nature of dovetail cutting. A very sharp chisel, held at a shallow angle, can slice the fibers cleanly rather than tearing them. Also, ensure you are consistently cutting with the grain where possible, or at least making very careful passes against the grain to minimize tear-out. Consider experimenting with a dedicated dovetail saw before switching to chisels for the initial cuts.",
      "source": "target",
