    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I've already honed the chisels to a mirror finish, so I'm pretty sure the sharpness isn't the main culprit. I also think the moisture content in the walnut might be a bit high since it's been a humid week. Do you think the wood being a bit 'active' or damp could be causing the fibers to compress and then snap instead of cutting? Or is there something else about the physical structure of the wood I should be looking at?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Wood absorbing moisture can definitely soften the fibers, making them more prone to tearing or compressing under the pressure of the chisel. If the moisture content is high, letting the walnut acclimatize and dry out to a stable interior humidity level is a crucial first step. Additionally, overly dense or figured walnut grain can resist cutting; use very light, controlled passes instead of applying heavy force to keep the pressure gentle.",
      "source": "target",
