    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'll try letting them sit in the shop for a few days to stabilize. One more thing though—I've noticed some of the splintering seems almost random, like it's happening even when I'm following the grain perfectly. It feels almost like the wood is fighting back or behaving inconsistently in ways that don't quite align with the physical grain patterns I'm seeing. Is it possible for the internal structure to have hidden weaknesses, or am I just missing some subtle technical detail in how I'm approaching the cuts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is possible the wood possesses internal structural anomalies, such as small checks or knots that are invisible to the eye, which act as weak points. Sometimes, the tension from the drying process creates stress points, even if the wood appears sound. Even when following the grain, extremely slight pressure variations or inconsistent knife angles can cause random tear-out. Re-evaluating your cutting technique with extreme patience, focusing on letting the sharp edge do the work, will likely resolve these sporadic issues.",
      "source": "target",
